  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHeavy showers and thunderstorms continue to track along an\ninstability gradient along the southeastern forecast area border\nearly this morning, largely driven by a series of weak mid- \nlevel disturbances atop low level moisture convergence. This \nforcing and the associated precipitation should continue to \nwane heading into early this morning. Otherwise, expect \npersistent low cloudiness in weak northeasterly flow within the \npost- frontal airmass for much of the day today, with high \ntemperatures largely struggling to rise much above 70 degrees\nacross the lower elevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAlthough precipitable water values will finally fall below 99th\npercentile climatology for late September today, dipping to 1.4\nto 1.6 inches, ascent driven mainly by low-level convergence\nnear an inverted surface trough will still yield highly\nefficient and nearly stationary showers and thunderstorms by\nthis afternoon. These storms will initially manifest near the \nhigher terrain before very slowly drifting west through the \nlate afternoon and evening. While not all locations in the \nexisting Flash Flood Watch will see heavy rain, sufficient \nconfidence remains in locally heavy rainfall occurring over a \npatchwork of soils compromised by recent heavy rainfall to \nmaintain the current Flash Flood Watch which goes through\nWednesday morning.\n\nAnother round of locally moderate to heavy rainfall will be\npossible Wednesday into Wednesday night as a weak cold-core low\naloft settles overhead. Precipitable water values falling to 1.2\nto 1.5 inches will once again temper maximum rainfall rates.\nHowever, given highly compromised soils across portions of\nsoutheast Ohio and northern West Virginia, an additional\nextension or expansion of the Flood Watch may become necessary\ninto Wednesday night or early Thursday morning. \n\nSevere weather is not expected either day due to lessened \nmoisture loading of updrafts and generally modest instability; \nhowever, wet soils may allow even sub-severe gusty winds\nassociated with storms to uproot trees. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAs low pressure well off the Atlantic seaboard continues to work\nnorth through the end of the week, much drier air sourced from\neastern Canada will progressively be dragged south into the\nregion. This will result in a welcome period of no precipitation\nas precipitable water values fall through the 1 inch mark.\nExpect near-normal daytime highs in the lower to mid 70s and\ncool overnight lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s. This dry and\npleasant pattern will last through the weekend and into early\nnext week.",
